            {
              "id": "9720add2-3a01-4aeb-af69-753f316b5585",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": "<em><strong>Wordplay &amp; the Discourse of Video Games: Analyzing Words, Design, &amp; Play</strong></em> was written by Christopher A. Paul, published by Routledge, February 15, 2012."
            },
            {
              "id": "1d757071-5e86-42e1-a2dc-6c8c7c6bf780",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
            },
            {
              "id": "77e5d4ea-fb74-4438-ba93-1d6992e0e19c",
              "type": "quote",
              "html": "<em>In this timely new book, Christopher Paul analyzes how the words we use to talk about video games and the structures that are produced within games shape a particular way of gaming by focusing on how games create meaning, lead to identification and division, persuade, and circulate ideas. Paul examines the broader social discourse about gaming, including: the way players are socialized into games; the impact of the lingering association of video games as kid's toys; the dynamics within specific games (including </em>Grand Theft Auto<em> and EA Sports Games); and the ways in which players participate in shaping the discourse of games, demonstrated through examples like the reward system of </em>World of Warcraft <em>and the development of theorycraft. Overall, this book illustrates how video games are shaped by words, design and play; all of which are negotiated, ongoing practices among the designers, players, and society that construct the discourse of video games.</em>"
            },
            {
              "id": "b6730c72-2cfa-47c4-820e-1cd22768efdb",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
            }
          ]
